</nav></header><main id="main"><div class="wrap"><section class="case-hero"><a class="back" href="index.html#work">← All selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">Delil AI / Lead discovery · CRM workflow</p><h1>Keep the context close to the lead.</h1><p class="case-intro">Structuring a sales workspace where people can browse leads, review details and configure the information they need.</p><dl class="case-meta"><div><dt>Product</dt><dd>Lead discovery & CRM</dd></div><div><dt>My focus</dt><dd>UX/UI · Information architecture</dd></div><div><dt>Core complexity</dt><dd>Records, details & configuration</dd></div><div><dt>Deliverables</dt><dd>Navigation · Tables · Side panels</dd></div></dl></section><div class="case-cover"><img src="assets/dalil3.png" alt="Delil CRM lead table with contextual contact information side panels" fetchpriority="high"></div><div class="case-layout"><aside class="case-toc" aria-label="On this page"><p>On this page</p><a href="#challenge">01 / The question</a><a href="#structure">02 / The structure</a><a href="#decisions">03 / Design decisions</a><a href="#outcome">04 / Deliverables & next steps</a></aside><article class="case-content"><section id="challenge"><h2>A record is only part of the decision.</h2><p>A list of leads is useful for scanning and comparing. Deciding what to do with a lead needs more context: contact information, company details and related activity. The interface needs to support both levels of work.</p><p><strong>The design question:</strong> how can a person inspect a lead without losing their place in the larger list?</p></section><section id="structure"><h2>Map the wider workspace.</h2><p>The product architecture connects account access, administrative flows, integrations and the lead workspace. Mapping those areas creates a framework for decisions inside the table and detail view.</p><figure><button class="image-button" data-zoom aria-label="Expand image: Overview of Delil product structure and interface flows"><img src="assets/dalil1.png" alt="Overview of Delil product structure and interface flows" loading="lazy"></button><figcaption>The wider product structure around the lead-management experience.<span>Click to expand ↗</span></figcaption></figure></section><section id="decisions"><h2>Make room for detail without losing the list.</h2><div class="decision"><small>Decision 01</small><h3>Open the detail beside the work.</h3><p>The lead detail appears in a side panel, preserving the surrounding table. A person can inspect a record and return to the list without rebuilding their context.</p><p class="tradeoff"><strong>The trade-off:</strong> The side panel has limited width. Longer content needs a clear hierarchy and a deliberate small-screen layout.</p></div><figure><button class="image-button" data-zoom aria-label="Expand image: Lead detail panels beside the CRM table"><img src="assets/dalil3.png" alt="Lead detail panels beside the CRM table" loading="lazy"></button><figcaption>Contextual panels let the record and the surrounding list remain visible.<span>Click to expand ↗</span></figcaption></figure><div class="decision"><small>Decision 02</small><h3>Let the table reflect the task.</h3><p>Column configuration gives people control over which fields are visible. This accommodates different ways of reviewing and comparing leads.</p><p class="tradeoff"><strong>The trade-off:</strong> Flexibility can increase setup effort. Useful default columns and a predictable way to reset the view remain important.</p></div><figure><button class="image-button" data-zoom aria-label="Expand image: Configuration options within the Delil workspace"><img src="assets/dalil2.png" alt="Configuration options within the Delil workspace" loading="lazy"></button><figcaption>Configuration controls within the product interface.<span>Click to expand ↗</span></figcaption></figure><div class="decision"><small>Decision 03</small><h3>Use a consistent frame for related tasks.</h3><p>The navigation, table area and detail surfaces establish a shared structure across the workspace. Repeating that frame keeps attention on the changing content.</p><p class="tradeoff"><strong>The trade-off:</strong> Consistency should not force every task into the same pattern. Complex creation or editing work may need more space than a panel provides.</p></div></section><section id="outcome"><h2>A connected lead workspace.</h2><ul class="deliverables"><li>A mapped structure for account, administrative and product workflows.</li><li>Lead tables with contextual detail panels.</li><li>Configuration patterns for adapting the workspace to the task.</li></ul><div class="learning"><p>The interface should help people move between overview and detail while preserving their place.</p></div><h3>What I’d test next</h3><p>I would test finding a relevant lead, comparing two records and returning to the previous table position. I would also check keyboard navigation, panel dismissal and the usefulness of default columns. These are proposed evaluations, not validated outcomes.</p></section></article></div><a class="next-project" href="lillup.html"><div><p class="eyebrow">Next project</p><h3>Lillup</h3></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><section class="contact" id="contact"><div class="wrap contact-layout"><div><p class="eyebrow">A good place to start</p><h2>What are you<br>trying to solve?</h2><p>I’m open to product design roles and focused projects with teams building useful, thoughtful software.</p></div><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="mailto:tatheerulhassan799@gmail.com">Start a conversation <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section></main>
